  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?",
  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=-1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-1$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it  (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=3$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=3$  points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/6a05aa0192d5edcb3181e44a5e3c15f5514372c8c50f105e9c4bbae40d660118.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/06a3d9118f9e3c09f374760a6e8209b33c9458e4d4b476c56fae3c57f2ca3fbe.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=-1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line.",
